<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Minden</h1><p class="st-meta">1648 CE – 1807 CE · capital Minden</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Secularised Westphalian bishopric on the Weser that became a Prussian principality and gave its name to a famous battle of 1759.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> around 800, had turned Protestant in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 it was secularised and assigned to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg</a> as part of its compensation for ceding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Western Pomerania</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a> governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a> through a provincial administration, later combined with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-ravensberg">County of Ravensberg</a> in a single chamber of war and domains. The town was fortified and garrisoned, and the region produced grain and linen. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> an Anglo-German army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-of-brunswick-wolfenbuttel">Ferdinand of Brunswick</a> defeated the French nearby at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-minden">Battle of Minden</a> on 1 August 1759.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia's</a> collapse in 1806, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a> was divided between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-westphalia">Kingdom of Westphalia</a> and, from 1810, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In 1815 it returned to Prussia as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-westphalia">Province of Westphalia</a>; the area is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-minden.jpg" alt="Principality of Minden" width="320" height="290"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_Prince-Bishopric_of_Minden_(1560).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1648 CE – 1807 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Minden</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frederick William, the Great Elector, Frederick II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fürstentum Minden</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1728&amp;focus=principality-of-minden" aria-label="Principality of Minden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4880 373 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Minden lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5200 629L5199 628M5200 629L5282 625M5200 629L5300 622M5200 629L5215 656"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5199 628h0M5282 625h0M5300 622h0M5215 656h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5200 629h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5200 629h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Minden. <a href="/atlas?year=1728&amp;focus=principality-of-minden">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
